    "lawCode": "Amd §4-b, Chap 802 of 1947",
    "lawSection": "Port Authority of New York and New Jersey",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4263\n\nSPONSOR: WEBB\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 802 of the laws of 1947, relating to facilitat-\ning the financing and effectuation of air terminals by the Port of New\nYork Authority, in relation to information concerning services for human\ntrafficking victims in lactation rooms\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require Port Authority airports to provide information concerning\nservices for human trafficking victims in lactation rooms.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 4-b of chapter 802 of 1947, as added by chapter\n388 of 2022 to require Port Authority airports to make informational\ncards or signs available in any lactation rooms, which contain informa-\ntion on services for trafficking victims, including the national human\ntrafficking hotline number.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHuman trafficking is a global phenomenon that generates $150 billion in\nannual profits. In 2021, an estimated 25 million people were trafficked.\nThe convenience and anonymity of airports makes it convenient for traf-\nfickers to move victims.\n\nBecause airports are known hubs used to traffic victims both interna-\ntionally and domestically, they are an ideal location to provide infor-\nmation about victim services. This information can not only inform\nvictims, but also other travelers about the availability of services.\nMany victims are heavily supervised and controlled while in transit,\npreventing them from safely seeking help or finding resources without\nfear of retaliation or threat.\n\nChapter 388 of 2022 required Port Authority airports to visibly post\nhuman trafficking informational signs or cards in its public restrooms.\nThis legislation would require the signs or cards to also be posted in\nany lactation rooms.\n\nThrough this legislation, airports can assume a more prominent role in\nresponding to and preventing human trafficking. It will also afford\nvictims the tools and resources to hopefully escape their captors.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
